<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Tadmekka</h1><p class="st-meta">850 CE – 1377 CE (approx.) · capital Tadmekka</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Berber caravan town in Mali's Adrar des Ifoghas, home to the oldest Arabic inscriptions in West Africa.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Tadmekka, at the site of Essouk in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adrar-des-ifoghas">Adrar des Ifoghas</a> of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, grew from the 9th century as a market on the trans-Saharan routes linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-bend">Niger bend</a> with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sultanate-of-ouargla">Ouargla</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghadames">Ghadames</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a>. Its inhabitants were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a>, and Ibadi merchants were among its first Muslim traders.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The 11th-century geographer al-Bakri described Tadmekka as a fine town whose name meant 'resembling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>', ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> chiefs, with merchants who used unstamped pure gold as currency. Excavations have found clay coin moulds for gold dinars, and Arabic inscriptions on nearby rocks, dated 1011 and 1013, are the oldest known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Tadmekka declined as trade shifted towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao">Gao</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a> extended its influence. By the late 14th century the town was largely abandoned, and its people dispersed among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/emirate-of-adrar">Adrar</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 850 CE – 1377 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:76.62%;width:10.49%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>850 CE – 1377 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tadmekka</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Berber</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mali">Mali</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Essouk, Tadmakka</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1114&amp;focus=tadmekka" aria-label="Tadmekka on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4712 1482 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tadmekka lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5032 1738h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5032 1738h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1114&amp;focus=tadmekka">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

